CHECK:BRACES: Blank lines aren't necessary after an open brace '{'
#253: FILE: tools/testing/selftests/timers/set-timer-lat.c:184:
+	for (clock_id = 0; clock_id < NR_CLOCKIDS; clock_id++) {
+

The placement of braces is stylistically incorrect.
The preferred way is to put the opening brace last on the line,
and put the closing brace first::

  if (x is true) {
          we do y
  }

This applies for all non-functional blocks.
However, there is one special case, namely functions: they have the
opening brace at the beginning of the next line, thus::

  int function(int x)
  {
          body of function
  }

See: https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/latest/process/coding-style.html#placing-braces-and-spaces

CHECK:UNNECESSARY_PARENTHESES: Unnecessary parentheses around 'clock_id == CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID'
#254: FILE: tools/testing/selftests/timers/set-timer-lat.c:185:
+		if ((clock_id == CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID) ||
+				(clock_id == CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID) ||
+				(clock_id == CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW) ||
+				(clock_id == CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE) ||
+				(clock_id == CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE) ||
+				(clock_id == CLOCK_HWSPECIFIC))

Parentheses are not required in the following cases:

  1. Function pointer uses::

      (foo->bar)();

    could be::

      foo->bar();

  2. Comparisons in if::

      if ((foo->bar) && (foo->baz))
      if ((foo == bar))

    could be::

      if (foo->bar && foo->baz)
      if (foo == bar)

  3. addressof/dereference single Lvalues::

      &(foo->bar)
      *(foo->bar)

    could be::

      &foo->bar
      *foo->bar

